WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 123, it's July 6th, 2026.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:12.540
Starting the week with the latest from space and science, an asteroid set for a once-in-a-millenium

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.860
Earth flyby could be seen by 90% of the world's population.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.420
Asteroid apophis will pass unusually close to Earth during its 2029 approach.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.240
The encounter offers a rare chance to study a near-earth object at close range without

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.280
a dedicated mission.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.800
Ground-based telescopes worldwide will track the asteroid's path and surface properties

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.180
as it sweeps past.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:41.600
The event ranks as one of the most accessible asteroid flybys in recorded history.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.680
Observers are already planning coordinated campaigns to measure any changes in the asteroid's

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.440
rotation or shape induced by Earth's gravity.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:57.400
Follow-up observations after 2029 will test models of how such objects evolve over repeated

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.640
planetary encounters.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:05.200
Now shifting focus from Earth's gravitational influence on a passing asteroid to China's

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.160
first dedicated mission to collect material from one.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:15.140
90% of the world's population could glimpse asteroid apophis during its 2029 flyby.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:21.480
Ground telescopes will measure any tidal distortion or surface changes caused by Earth's gravity

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.100
at a distance of only a few Earth radii.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:30.540
The geometry allows repeated observations over several nights from widely separated longitudes.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.400
Coordinated radar and optical campaigns will refine the asteroid's shape model and spin

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.440
state to unprecedented precision.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.540
The data will test predictions of how close encounters alter near-earth objects over time.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.040
What surface features might shift enough for even modest amateur telescopes to detect

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.160
after the flyby.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:58.700
If Earth were scaled to the size of a basketball in downtown Toronto, apophis on its 2029

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.800
approach would pass closer than the nearest subway stop.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:08.280
Title forces during such an encounter can torque the asteroid's rotation and fracture loosely

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.440
bound surface material without breaking the body apart.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:18.440
The relative speed reaches tens of kilometers per second, so the entire gravitational interaction

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:24.060
lasts only minutes, yet deposits enough energy to alter spin periods by measurable fractions.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:30.600
Precise tracking after the flyby will reveal whether the asteroid's rotation rate changed by even

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:36.900
a few percent. The open question is whether repeated passages through the same gravitational keyhole

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:41.080
could eventually steer the orbit onto a collision course centuries later.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:47.540
China's Tianwen 2 spacecraft reached the near-earth asteroid on schedule and returned the first

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:53.780
close-up images. The probe revealed a small, elongated rocky body roughly a few hundred meters

00:02:53.780 --> 00:03:00.380
across. Tianwen 2 launched in May 2025 and will collect samples for return to Earth.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.780
The mission marks China's first attempt at asteroid sample return.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.140
Scientists will analyze the material to compare it with meteorites already on Earth.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.340
Next steps include mapping the surface in detail before the sampling sequence begins.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.580
While one mission studies an asteroid up close, another telescope is revealing the

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:26.520
universe's earliest giant black holes. The European Space Agency's Euclid Observatory

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:32.240
detected 31 quasars shining when the universe was just 670 million years old.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:38.800
Two of these objects rank as the earliest quasars yet observed, each powered by a supermassive

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:45.260
black hole. The light from these sources equals that of a trillion suns. Euclid's wide field

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.580
infrared observations allowed the team to sift through millions of galaxies to isolate these

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:56.440
rare bright cores. The detections pushed the timeline for the formation of the first giant black

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:02.560
holes earlier than many models predicted. Further spectra from ground-based telescopes will help

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:09.040
measure the black holes masses and growth rates. From the first supermassive black holes to a nearby

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:15.820
world whose habitability outlook has improved. Astronomers used the hobby-everly telescope to

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:22.340
re-examine an exoplanet previously flagged as potentially too active for life. Refined measurements

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:27.120
of the planet's radius and mass indicate a denser composition than first thought.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.960
The data suggests the world retains a substantial rocky component beneath any atmosphere.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.480
Researchers now plan additional transit observations to search for atmospheric gases.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.040
The updated parameters move the planet closer to the range considered Earth-like in bulk

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:50.140
properties. Follow-up studies will focus on whether the atmosphere shows signs of water vapor or

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:56.240
other volatiles. From individual worlds to an entire compact system shaped by a brown dwarf.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:04.520
An international team has characterized T-O-I-201-C, the longest period transiting brown dwarf with a

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:10.680
measured mass. The object sits in a co-planar system where its eccentric orbit influences the

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:17.480
stability of inner planets. Data from multiple observatories including ESO and ENF facilities

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:23.520
pinned down the brown dwarfs mass and orbital elements. The configuration challenges standard

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:29.660
models of how massive companions affect smaller worlds. Continued monitoring will test whether

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:36.000
additional planets exist beyond the current known set. The system provides a laboratory for studying

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.620
dynamical interactions over long-time scales. From dynamical puzzles to a high-speed rehearsal for

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:50.280
planetary defense. Jack says Hayabusa 2 spacecraft passed within a few kilometers of the 450 meter

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:56.720
asteroid at roughly 5 kilometers per second. The high-speed encounter gathered imaging and spectral

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:02.860
data on the object's surface and shape. Mission scientists aim to refine techniques for future

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:09.240
planetary defense scenarios. The fly-by adds to the spacecraft's extended mission after its successful

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:16.100
Ryugu sample return. Analysis of the new images will help calibrate models of asteroid regolith

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:22.500
behavior. Further encounters are planned as the probe continues its journey. From asteroid defense

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:28.680
to seasonal weather on Mars, the European orbiter captured multiple dust devils swirling across

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:35.280
one Martian valley during a recent imaging sequence. In Mars' thin atmosphere, the vortices can reach

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:42.380
heights of 8 kilometers and speeds near 45 meters per second. The observations show how solar heating

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:48.980
lifts fine dust high into the air, redistributing material across the surface. Repeated imaging

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:55.740
passes allow scientists to track changes in dust-devil frequency with season. The data feed into global

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:01.940
circulation models used to predict dust storms. Future orbits will target other regions to build a

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:07.980
broader statistical sample. From Mars' weather to a nearby galaxy behaving like those in the early

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:15.080
universe. An international team tracked a radio-bright galaxy 1.8 billion light years away that has

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:21.040
maintained elevated activity for more than 8 years. The central black hole is a creating fresh

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:27.720
material and launching a high-energy particle jet. Archival and new observations show the jet remains

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:33.940
powered long after the initial trigger. The system offers a nearby analog for the rapid black hole

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:40.120
growth seen at higher red shifts. Continued monitoring will measure how the jets' power and direction

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:46.100
